    summary:
      "A /26 mask means the last octet is 11000000 (128+64 = 192). Block size = 256 − 192 = 64.\nSubnets jump in steps of 64: .0, .64, .128, .192 (stop at 256). Each is a network address.\nFor the .64 block: broadcast = 64 + 64 − 1 = .127, usable hosts = .65 to .126.\nAddress .96 falls between .64 and .127 — this is the correct subnet.\n\nWHY THE OTHER ANSWERS ARE WRONG:\n\n192.168.1.32/27\n/27 mask = 11100000 (128+64+32 = 224). Block size = 256 − 224 = 32.\nSubnets jump every 32. The .32 block: network .32, broadcast = 32 + 32 − 1 = .63, usable = .33 to .62.\nAddress .96 is outside this range.\n\n192.168.1.32/28\n/28 mask = 11110000 (128+64+32+16 = 240). Block size = 256 − 240 = 16.\nSubnets jump every 16: .0, .16, .32, .48, .64...\nThe .32 block: broadcast = 32 + 16 − 1 = .47, usable = .33 to .46.\nAddress .96 is far outside this range.\n\n192.168.1.64/29\n/29 mask = 11111000 (128+64+32+16+8 = 248). Block size = 256 − 248 = 8.\nSubnets jump every 8: .64, .72, .80, .88, .96...\nThe .64 block: broadcast = 64 + 8 − 1 = .71, usable = .65 to .70.\nAddress .96 is outside this range — it falls in its own .96 block under this mask.",
