    question:
      "When a switch configuration includes a user-defined error threshold on a per-port basis, to which switching method will the switch revert when the error threshold is reached?",
    options: [
      "cut-through",
      "store-and-forward",
      "fast-forward",
      "fragment-free",
    ],
    correct: [1],
    explanation: "",
    summary:
      "Cut-through switching is very fast — the switch begins forwarding a frame as soon as it reads the destination MAC address (first 6 bytes). It does NOT wait for the full frame and does NOT check for errors.\nThis means corrupt frames may be forwarded. Store-and-forward switching waits for the ENTIRE frame to arrive, calculates and verifies the FCS (Frame Check Sequence) for errors, then forwards only valid frames. When the error rate on a port exceeds a configured threshold, the switch automatically switches from cut-through to store-and-forward mode to stop forwarding corrupt frames.",
    opt_explanations: [
      "WRONG ✗ — Cut-through is the FASTER method the switch is moving AWAY from when too many errors are detected.",
      "CORRECT ✓ — Store-and-forward checks every frame for errors (FCS). When the error threshold is reached, the switch reverts to this method to stop forwarding corrupt frames.",
      "WRONG ✗ — Fast-forward is a variant of cut-through — it forwards immediately after reading the destination MAC. Does not check for errors.",
      "WRONG ✗ — Fragment-free reads the first 64 bytes before forwarding (to avoid forwarding collision fragments). Still a cut-through variant — does not do full FCS checking.",
    ],
